{"step_index":365,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-21T21:55:58Z","thinking":"The user's request to \"completely remove\" something refers to the \"Qwen\" identity baked into the model. Previously I explained that methods such as fine-tuning or surgical weight editing could remove it. Now I need to consider which of these methods is best.\n\n\nConsidering various techniques, targeted concept erasure via weight surgery seems promising; it aims for complete removal by orthogonal projection. Negative DPO could also suppress the target tokens by maximizing their loss. Logit biasing offers an even more direct approach, zeroing the probabilities of specific tokens at the generation stage.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"machine unlearning\" erase identity from LLM weights \"who are you\" 2025 2026","toolAction":"Searching machine unlearning for LLM identity","toolSummary":"Search machine unlearning techniques for LLM identity"}}]}
{"step_index":366,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-21T21:55:58Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-22T03:25:58+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-22T03:26:05+05:30\nThe search for \"\"machine unlearning\" erase identity from LLM weights \"who are you\" 2025 2026\" returned the following summary:\n\"Machine unlearning\" is a field of research focused on selectively removing the influence of specific data, knowledge, or behaviors from a model's weights without requiring a full, costly retrain. As of 2025–2026, the industry has shifted toward viewing unlearning as a technical challenge that is **far from bulletproof**[1][2][3].\n\n### Key Challenges in Erasing \"Identity\"\n*   **Suppression vs. Erasure:** Research indicates that many current \"unlearning\" techniques function more like **suppression** than true erasure[4][5]. While a model may be trained to refuse the question \"Who are you?\" or to exhibit \"feigned ignorance,\" the underlying knowledge often remains encoded in the weights and can be recovered or \"relearned\" through adversarial probing or fine-tuning[1][5][6].\n*   **The \"Who Are You?\" Problem:** If an LLM has been trained to adopt a specific identity (e.g., a persona or brand-specific identity), this identity is deeply embedded in its weights rather than stored as a simple data point. Removing it is difficult because identity is often tied to the model's general reasoning and stylistic patterns rather than a single \"fact\" that can be neatly excised[7][8].\n*   **Reversibility:** Studies published in 2026 highlight the \"reversibility\" of unlearning[5]. Even when a model appears to have successfully forgotten information, minimal fine-tuning or specific input triggers can often restore the \"forgotten\" behavior, proving that the information was never truly deleted from the parameter space[5][9].\n\n### Current State of the Field (2025–2026)\n*   **Terminology Critique:** Recent academic discourse (including 2026 position papers) argues that \"machine unlearning\" is an overused term[8][10]. Experts suggest reserving the term specifically for \"dataset-defined deletion\" (where the result is indistinguishable from retraining) and using terms like **alignment, suppression, or editing** for other methods that merely alter behavior[8][10].\n*   **Auditing and Verification:** Because it is difficult to prove that information has been truly deleted, researchers are increasingly focused on **auditing frameworks**[11][12]. These methods use statistical testing or representation-level analysis (like CKA or PCA distance) to see if a model's internal activations truly differ from a version that never saw the data[11][5][12].\n*   **Practical Limitations:** While techniques like **RMU (Removal of Model Weights)** or token-level noise (e.g., **PERMUtok**) are being explored to localize forgetting, they often face trade-offs between \"catastrophic forgetting\" (where the model loses general capability) and \"shallow unlearning\" (where the model remains vulnerable to bypasses)[13][5][14].\n\n### Summary for Implementation\nIf your goal is to force an LLM to \"forget\" its identity, you are currently limited by the fact that:\n1.  **System Prompts are not weights:** Using a system prompt to change how a model identifies itself is easily bypassed via jailbreaking[7][15].\n2.  **Weight-based unlearning is experimental:** Attempting to surgically remove an \"identity\" from the weights is likely to result in either an incomplete removal (the model still \"knows\" who it is) or a degradation in the model's general performance[16][2].\n\nFor high-stakes applications, experts currently recommend combining **guardrail systems** (monitoring I/O) with **rigorous fine-tuning** to steer the model, while acknowledging that complete, irreversible erasure of complex concepts like identity remains a significant unsolved research problem[15][5][3].\n\nSources:\n[1] [modulai.io](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFcS6rM_xwG8FNiSZ3cTo2qw_fjYdQX3oUdIpR0_fDDnEBHiP4gJN-RA4BjzUr9sVx0eYOiHG0rVbTeSYwm675KM0U9aR5yBVmmfpmqbsayOI4SEVGP9Vh0qGyTfIbSwqctdETYXyMVK1gFcNiE0feQjz1LplbqxkwfP218)\n[2] [medium.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEv5Lcya_lyoLQWzt7b7it0YDoRH8S9xabHpV_BKk4WKHhXNU_XoLjGvKGYVuV8lFW6mVOFqstdG6yGR209slVxO-q1-LzyukHax8B_w0tenvIVtucgBgGtgMOn4p6Dz4jJbLWjb5T6-CCDnBPZo8JVH5CSl_JF-EtU2wDMmzzAHm48L0WBx-xug7k=)\n[3] [microsoft.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHeNsWI2TMSY1AAb70m9JAT2BKyRt6PvwGAs0sfruvMGRFWa4c9qt3Y_l3b--gbkWaithdK0ANWo0Rp-Xsr3WEHJENq1p_JYcymtn9E_qf8zhNQ6fNhtMpQpZaYNZ4zeNEPJojrknx1ivTh78tZbR1RT0JqQAPgdvetSIe0B1EJniuGwCsQ7bbyQx5ukNRcTOVWQFb4rhY7aDixY5hT0T_cEuYvIw==)\n[4] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHwOatjN5IIo9IHyCV_TM4aMLV8RqCu8jh9EiS0CJM2XBcKtEvhB2koef_LIkliHOrpGKC7tHGTSw8Thp1Ab0G8iYcz-EieGDcQdNDcdUVmj8RgYo9iTw==)\n[5] [icml.cc](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFfAK6vcBskfDx9vVgNkbiCBIjWUgv9Ac8V3o4jw5wo82NQmRkPaTvegv6ewKSFG-a7H5wYt2LwaZe4X_q-6ZWMwqozeoLyOlHV4pOYJcmZPoohEolwdLsW1D9dMbhVlg==)\n[6] [ibm.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFY7As6bAdA8HNbZrjxgYZZL6e63jw0SXTdrfLtZoSsvoHlydj0PyL-5P6gDAVr0-8ztifDF9Pbt5Dj6dENvVdRXWnQ63DxFOHYpl6f2LUw2R1jKBIJNbbORS-cJRIr4HlpamtuhiBnDyPJ3kmVdr4gIvB6ywuVbgNk4MaFh5-e3qqY-2-Ml0KDX8Oa4l0EszlX2kzDMExn)\n[7] [ibm.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQECDTMYHdihZvprjLvDZ4npyzOXFlrs-cG4dl-9IVKxP4ge_idncdigxzLna_6jdCWgvtp5uyi69UlRCpj-HusM5QTbvsXYJSBqElzrdJ1ZuLfPBZiTF5pffik7FHuDNM4uAw==)\n[8] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG7o2OTdEnbt5ViHoe5BlkYBPppPIS1D21IrtwcDYgWrg9q4FhXnuSW7diUTz6T7pj6wZIcX16qXegfO8Dp4owsVOGfn2XZiz01J8zlysQ8n0GkfQey_A==)\n[9] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/groundi